-- Derived from wecon_wtest/WECON-02.DATA, with the WECON/WTEST economic-limit
-- testing removed and the following changes, so that the drawdown limit rather
-- than well shut-in is what the case measures:
--
--   * a skin of 20 on both producers, lowering the productivity index from
--     ~360 to ~75 sm3/day/bar so that the drawdown at the rate targets lands
--     in a realistic 5-30 bar range rather than 1-3 bar;
--   * a second water injector 'E-2H' in the western fault compartment, which
--     in WECON-02 has no pressure support at all;
--   * both injectors put on a 300 bar BHP limit so they self-regulate to the
--     voidage the producers need.  Without this the producers deplete to the
--     75 bar BHP limit and shut, and a shut well cannot demonstrate a
--     drawdown limit.
--
-- WELDRAW-MSW-BASE is the BASE CASE for WELDRAW-01-MSW: the same model,
-- with the producer D-2H as a multisegment well (one vertical branch, one
-- segment per perforated layer), but without the WELDRAW keyword.  Both
-- producers follow their WCONPROD targets, with the natural drawdown left
-- free; as in WELDRAW-BASE, D-2H moves onto its 3000 sm3/day liquid limit
-- (WMCTL 1 -> 4) on 1 Mar 2019 of its own accord.
--
-- WELDRAW-MSW-BASE and WELDRAW-01-MSW are identical apart from the
-- WELDRAW keyword, so their summary vectors can be compared step for step
-- to isolate the effect of the drawdown limit on a multisegment well.
--
-- Segment node depths are the cell-centre depths of column (4,2,1..4);
-- connection MD ranges are the layer boundaries of the same column.

--
-- To verify the drawdown limit from the summary output: WUDD is the drawdown
-- (defined as WBP - WBHP), so it should sit on the WELDRAW value while WMCTL
-- reports the rate control the limit imposes (4 = LRAT, 3 = GRAT).  The rate
-- divided by the productivity index, WLPR / WPIL for a liquid target phase or
-- WGPR / WPIG for a gas one, reproduces the same limit, since the limit enters
-- as Qmax = Dmax * sum_j(Tw_j * M_j).

